  "notes": [
   "The part list is the same as in step 1. Nothing was added or dropped.",
   "The motor controller board is not in the catalogue, so it stays a new type. The board plugs onto the first 26 GPIO pins of the Pi. The catalogue's raspberry-pi-5 only has a GPIO_HEADER port, so the join is board GPIO_HEADER to Pi GPIO_HEADER. The motor signals on GPIO 7, 8, 9 and 10 run through that header.",
   "Motor 1 is on terminal block A and motor 2 on B. Motor wires are symmetric, so the polarity of each motor is chosen in software (the pin order in the Robot() call). The tutorial's left/right labelling is also set in software.",
   "Battery holder red wire (positive) goes to the + terminal and black wire (negative) to the - terminal, as the manual says.",
   "The four AA cells sit in the holder in series, so the holder's positive and negative are connected through the chain of cells.",
   "The wheels are pushed onto the motor axles. The dc-motor catalogue entry has no axle port, so these joins have no motor port.",
   "The ball caster has no edges. The cardboard box chassis it is screwed to is not a part in this graph, so it has nothing to join to.",
   "The USB power bank powers the Pi through a USB cable to the Pi's USB-C power port. The usb-cable type has no ports."
  ]
